Subject: [PATCH 0005] update: read a static JSON feed instead of an update service
Refs: ADR-0010
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix.

INTENT
======

Point the update client at static JSON files behind a CDN, so that shipping
auto-updates requires no server to operate.

The delta already sets `updateUrl` to the feed's base
(apps/ide/branding/product.delta.jq). This patch is the client change that makes
the requests it issues satisfiable by files rather than by a service.

WHY STATIC JSON
===============

Upstream's update client talks to an update *service* that answers a query about
platform, architecture, quality, and the currently installed commit, and computes
what to offer. Operating that service means running, monitoring, and securing an
internet-facing endpoint whose failure mode is "nobody can update".

A project with one maintainer should not own that. Static JSON has no runtime, no
database, no deploy, and its worst failure is a cache serving a stale version for
an hour. It is the correct amount of infrastructure here, and it is why ADR-0010
lists this as a required patch rather than an optional nicety.

THE SHAPE
=========

One file per platform-architecture-quality triple:

  $updateUrl/api/update/win32-x64/stable/latest.json
  $updateUrl/api/update/win32-arm64/stable/latest.json
  $updateUrl/api/update/darwin-x64/stable/latest.json
  $updateUrl/api/update/darwin-arm64/stable/latest.json
  $updateUrl/api/update/linux-x64/stable/latest.json
  $updateUrl/api/update/linux-arm64/stable/latest.json

Each holding the fields upstream's client already expects — version, productVersion,
url, sha256hash, supportsFastUpdate — so the response *shape* needs no change. What
changes is that the client stops sending the installed commit as a query parameter
and stops expecting the server to decide, and instead fetches the current
descriptor and compares locally.

FILES
=====

  src/vs/platform/update/common/updateService.ts        (the URL construction)
  src/vs/platform/update/electron-main/abstractUpdateService.ts
  src/vs/platform/update/electron-main/updateService.win32.ts
  src/vs/platform/update/electron-main/updateService.darwin.ts
  src/vs/platform/update/electron-main/updateService.linux.ts

The per-platform files matter because the download-and-apply mechanisms differ.
macOS delegates to Squirrel.Mac, which fetches its own feed and needs its own
static file; Windows downloads an installer and runs it; Linux notifies and does
nothing else. Only the URL construction is genuinely shared.

RISK
====

Low to medium. The update platform is not a churn hotspot — it did not appear in
ADR-0010's measured table because it was not in the blast radius at all — but the
patch touches five files, so its exposure is breadth rather than volatility.

Reduce that breadth if possible: if the URL construction can be confined to
updateService.ts alone, this becomes a low-risk single-file patch.

MUST NOT BE SKIPPED
===================

A build with a broken update client is not merely missing a feature. It is a build
that cannot ship a security fix to the people already running it. If this patch is
not ready, the honest response is to disable update checking entirely rather than
to ship a client pointed at a feed that does not answer — a silently failing
updater looks identical to an up-to-date install.

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

  1. Write and publish one real feed file first, even by hand. A client patched
     against a guessed schema is untestable.
  2. Change URL construction and local comparison. Leave the response type alone.
  3. Verify on each platform separately. Squirrel.Mac in particular fails silently
     when its feed is wrong, so "no update offered" is not evidence of success.
  4. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine -- src/vs/platform/update \
       > apps/ide/patches/0005-update-client-static-json-feed.patch
